PUBLIC RESPONSE: shadow

The form that is the clearest non-case is shadow. 

Trisyllabic shortening / trisyllabic laxing is the historical shortening of a tense/stressed vowel in a word which has become three or more syllables in form, essentially stressed V → lax V / _C σ σ e.g. /iː/ > /ɪ/, /aɪ/ > /ɪ/, /iː/ > /ɛ/ etc.

* derivative < derive /dɪˈraɪv/ > /dɪˈrɪvətɪv/ : stressed /aɪ/ is laxed/shortened in the 4-syllable /-ative/ formation;
* serenity < serene /səˈriːn/ > /səˈrɛnəti/ : stressed /iː/ > /ɛ/ in the 4-syllable /-ity/ formation;
* pleasant is the classic disyllabic reflex of a historically trisyllabic *pleas-ant*, /pliːz/ > /ˈplɛzənt/: /iː/ > /ɛ/ under the laxing environment;
* gratitude and southern are the regular historical reflexes of their Latinate / -ern/ formations with a shortened/laxed initial stressed vowel, /ɡrætɪtjuːd/ < *grat-itudo and /ˈsʌðən/ < *suːðern/*southern*.
Shadow /ˈʃædoʊ/ is native Germanic *sceadu*. It is a disyllabic, root-level word with a short /æ/ already in its Old English source and it has never been built up by a suffix that creates the three-syllable trisyllabic laxing environment.  In the list southern and pleasant are also synchronically disyllabic, but shadow is the only item with no derived trisyllabic source at all.

